1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,700
The Selarang Square Squeeze, 1942

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:08,071
On the 2nd of September 1942,
somewhere between 15,000 and

3
00:00:08,151 --> 00:00:12,598
17,000 British and Australian
prisoners of war were marched

4
00:00:12,678 --> 00:00:16,282
into the barracks square
at Selarang, in Changi,

5
00:00:16,362 --> 00:00:21,350
and kept there until they agreed
to sign a promise not to escape.

6
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:24,975
The square had been built
for a single battalion.

7
00:00:24,975 --> 00:00:28,828
For the next four days the men
lived on it and on the roofs and

8
00:00:28,908 --> 00:00:32,887
stairs of the barrack blocks around
it, with a few taps for water

9
00:00:32,967 --> 00:00:37,150
and latrines dug through the parade
ground, while dysentery spread.

10
00:00:37,150 --> 00:00:40,995
One of them, an Australian
private named George Aspinall,

11
00:00:41,075 --> 00:00:45,264
photographed the square in secret
with a small folding camera,

12
00:00:45,344 --> 00:00:48,925
and his pictures are the
reason we can still see it.

13
00:00:48,925 --> 00:00:54,492
In the 1930s the British army built a
new garrison on the Changi peninsula,

14
00:00:54,572 --> 00:00:57,429
at the eastern tip of Singapore island,

15
00:00:57,509 --> 00:01:01,725
as part of the fortress that
guarded the new naval base.

16
00:01:01,725 --> 00:01:04,978
Selarang Barracks, a set
of three-storey blocks

17
00:01:05,058 --> 00:01:08,250
around a large parade
square, was part of it.

18
00:01:08,250 --> 00:01:13,288
By 1937 it was home to the 2nd
Battalion of the Gordon Highlanders,

19
00:01:13,368 --> 00:01:18,176
a Scottish regiment, and the
newspapers of the time reported its

20
00:01:18,256 --> 00:01:23,450
boxing contests and its accidents
as part of ordinary garrison life.

21
00:01:23,450 --> 00:01:27,263
When Singapore surrendered
on the 15th of February 1942,

22
00:01:27,343 --> 00:01:32,477
the British and Australian troops on
the island were marched out to Changi,

23
00:01:32,557 --> 00:01:37,075
and the garrison's barracks became
the main prisoner-of-war camp.

24
00:01:37,075 --> 00:01:40,172
The prisoners were left
largely to organise

25
00:01:40,252 --> 00:01:43,725
themselves inside it,
under their own officers.

26
00:01:43,725 --> 00:01:47,253
Many were sent out in
working parties, some of them

27
00:01:47,333 --> 00:01:51,286
to build the Syonan Jinja
shrine at MacRitchie Reservoir,

28
00:01:51,366 --> 00:01:54,125
which I wrote about in an earlier post.

29
00:01:54,125 --> 00:01:58,511
In August 1942 General Fukuye
Shimpei arrived at Changi

30
00:01:58,591 --> 00:02:02,975
to take charge of the
prisoner-of-war camps in Malaya.

31
00:02:02,975 --> 00:02:06,173
Four prisoners had escaped
and been recaptured:

32
00:02:06,253 --> 00:02:11,055
Corporal Rodney Breavington and
Private Victor Gale, both Australians,

33
00:02:11,135 --> 00:02:15,250
and Privates Harold Waters and
Eric Fletcher, both British.

34
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:19,147
Fukuye ordered every prisoner
to sign a form that read,

35
00:02:19,227 --> 00:02:24,000
in part, "I will not, under any
circumstances, attempt to escape."

36
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,750
Almost all of them refused.

37
00:02:26,750 --> 00:02:30,402
Their officers held that a
prisoner of war had a duty

38
00:02:30,482 --> 00:02:34,274
to try to escape, and that
a promise extracted by force

39
00:02:34,354 --> 00:02:37,875
went against the international
rules on prisoners.

40
00:02:37,875 --> 00:02:42,055
On the 30th of August Fukuye
ordered all the British and

41
00:02:42,135 --> 00:02:46,700
Australian prisoners who had
refused into Selarang Barracks.

42
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:49,592
The men moved in on the 2nd of September,

43
00:02:49,672 --> 00:02:51,775
carrying whatever they could.

44
00:02:51,775 --> 00:02:55,708
Michael Hubert, an Australian
private who kept a diary,

45
00:02:55,788 --> 00:02:58,925
described the column
as twelve people deep.

46
00:02:58,925 --> 00:03:03,160
The barrack blocks filled first,
and the rest of the men camped in

47
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:07,425
the open square under groundsheets
and blankets strung on poles.

48
00:03:07,425 --> 00:03:09,253
According to Hubert,

49
00:03:09,333 --> 00:03:13,150
each barrack block held about 1,200 men.

50
00:03:13,150 --> 00:03:15,800
Water was the first problem.

51
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:18,745
There were only three
taps for the whole square,

52
00:03:18,825 --> 00:03:21,958
and each prisoner was
allowed about a gallon a day,

53
00:03:22,038 --> 00:03:25,000
which went mostly on
drinking and cooking rice.

54
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,049
There were no latrines, so on
the first night the men dug two

55
00:03:29,129 --> 00:03:33,720
trenches through the middle of the
parade ground, each about four and

56
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:38,200
a half metres deep, and over the
next four days they dug 22 more.

57
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:40,816
Firewood was so scarce that the barrack

58
00:03:40,896 --> 00:03:43,800
doors were broken up to fuel the kitchens.

59
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:47,597
By the end of the first day
dysentery had broken out,

60
00:03:47,677 --> 00:03:51,700
and requests to move the sick
to hospital were refused.

61
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:55,642
Aspinall, a motor mechanic
from Parramatta in New South

62
00:03:55,722 --> 00:03:59,883
Wales who had come to Singapore
with the 2/30th Battalion,

63
00:03:59,963 --> 00:04:03,400
took his pictures with
a folding Kodak Brownie.

64
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:07,795
Photography was banned, so he
kept the camera hidden in a pocket

65
00:04:07,875 --> 00:04:12,350
of a belt he made himself and
developed the negatives in secret.

66
00:04:12,350 --> 00:04:15,681
Several of the Selarang
pictures were taken from the

67
00:04:15,761 --> 00:04:19,500
upper floors of the barracks,
looking down on the square.

68
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:23,086
On the 2nd of September,
the first day in the square,

69
00:04:23,166 --> 00:04:26,199
the four recaptured men
were shot by a firing

70
00:04:26,279 --> 00:04:29,600
squad on the beach at
Beting Kusah, near Changi.

71
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:33,452
The two senior prisoner officers,
Lieutenant Colonel E. B.

72
00:04:33,532 --> 00:04:37,452
Holmes of the British army and
Lieutenant Colonel Frederick

73
00:04:37,532 --> 00:04:41,125
Galleghan of the Australian
army, were made to watch.

74
00:04:41,125 --> 00:04:43,785
A witness, Lieutenant Colonel Frith,

75
00:04:43,865 --> 00:04:47,900
later described the shooting
as very badly organised.

76
00:04:47,900 --> 00:04:52,550
The four men are buried today
at the Kranji War Cemetery.

77
00:04:52,550 --> 00:04:55,340
With the sick list
growing and rations cut,

78
00:04:55,420 --> 00:05:00,225
the prisoners' commanders decided
they could not let the standoff go on.

79
00:05:00,225 --> 00:05:03,580
On the 4th of September
Holmes ordered every man to

80
00:05:03,660 --> 00:05:07,285
sign by eleven o'clock the
next morning, saying that he

81
00:05:07,365 --> 00:05:10,800
took the decision for the
sake of the men's health.

82
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:14,845
The men signed under duress,
and many used false names;

83
00:05:14,925 --> 00:05:18,975
Ned Kelly, the Australian
bushranger, was a favourite.

84
00:05:18,975 --> 00:05:21,664
On the 5th of September the men marched

85
00:05:21,744 --> 00:05:24,300
back to their own areas of the camp.

86
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:28,208
In April 1943 many of
them, Aspinall among them,

87
00:05:28,288 --> 00:05:32,775
were sent north to work on
the Burma-Thailand Railway.

88
00:05:32,775 --> 00:05:37,444
After the Japanese surrender,
Fukuye was tried at the Singapore

89
00:05:37,524 --> 00:05:42,646
War Crimes Court in February 1946,
on charges of ill-treating British

90
00:05:42,726 --> 00:05:47,400
and Australian prisoners and of
the execution of the four men.

91
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:51,076
The Straits Times reported
that the hearing on

92
00:05:51,156 --> 00:05:55,321
the 26th of February put
"the Selarang concentration

93
00:05:55,401 --> 00:05:59,075
of 17,000 prisoners"
under close examination.

94
00:05:59,075 --> 00:06:02,487
His defence argued that the
move to Selarang followed

95
00:06:02,567 --> 00:06:06,177
orders from the Japanese War
Ministry that all prisoners

96
00:06:06,257 --> 00:06:09,405
should sign such forms,
and that the shooting had

97
00:06:09,485 --> 00:06:13,175
been carried out by a subordinate
without his knowledge.

98
00:06:13,175 --> 00:06:17,950
He was found guilty on both
charges and sentenced to death.

99
00:06:17,950 --> 00:06:23,332
On Saturday the 27th of April 1946
he was shot by a firing squad on

100
00:06:23,412 --> 00:06:28,875
the beach at Changi, at the spot
where the four prisoners had died.

101
00:06:28,875 --> 00:06:33,756
As for Aspinall's photographs, in
early 1945 his commanding officer,

102
00:06:33,836 --> 00:06:37,039
Galleghan, ordered him
to hide the negatives,

103
00:06:37,119 --> 00:06:41,562
with other records, in a latrine
bore-hole inside Changi Gaol,

104
00:06:41,642 --> 00:06:45,800
and Galleghan returned most
of them to him after the war.

105
00:06:45,800 --> 00:06:49,252
Some negatives were also
kept in an Australian

106
00:06:49,332 --> 00:06:52,250
army water bottle with a false bottom.

107
00:06:52,250 --> 00:06:56,059
The water bottle and 202
of his images are now

108
00:06:56,139 --> 00:06:59,775
in the Australian War
Memorial in Canberra.

109
00:06:59,775 --> 00:07:02,733
After the war Selarang was a British and

110
00:07:02,813 --> 00:07:05,775
then an Australian army barracks again.

111
00:07:05,775 --> 00:07:10,829
In the early 1970s it was home
to the Australian units of ANZUK,

112
00:07:10,909 --> 00:07:14,438
the joint Australian,
New Zealand and British

113
00:07:14,518 --> 00:07:18,288
force that stayed on after
Britain withdrew most

114
00:07:18,368 --> 00:07:22,700
of its troops, until that
force was disbanded in 1974.

115
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:25,946
Today it is Selarang
Camp, a Singapore Armed

116
00:07:26,026 --> 00:07:29,125
Forces camp that is closed to the public.

117
00:07:29,125 --> 00:07:33,906
The story is told today at the
Changi Chapel and Museum on Upper

118
00:07:33,986 --> 00:07:39,150
Changi Road North, which is run by
the National Museum of Singapore.

119
00:07:39,150 --> 00:07:42,323
It was revamped and reopened in May 2021,

120
00:07:42,403 --> 00:07:46,529
and among its displays is
a Kodak Baby Brownie camera

121
00:07:46,609 --> 00:07:50,100
that a prisoner kept
hidden from the guards.

122
00:07:50,100 --> 00:07:54,240
Where it fits in the bigger
story: The Selarang square squeeze

123
00:07:54,320 --> 00:07:58,324
lasted four days and ended in
signatures given under duress,

124
00:07:58,404 --> 00:08:02,544
yet it is one of the best-recorded
episodes of the Occupation,

125
00:08:02,624 --> 00:08:06,300
because one prisoner risked
his life to photograph it.

126
00:08:06,300 --> 00:08:10,049
It shows how the prisoners
at Changi tried to hold on to

127
00:08:10,129 --> 00:08:13,399
their own rules as soldiers,
what that cost them,

128
00:08:13,479 --> 00:08:17,650
and how the trials after the
war tried to settle the account.

129
00:08:17,650 --> 00:08:21,769
Selarang Camp is still a working
barracks, closed to visitors,

130
00:08:21,849 --> 00:08:24,613
so Aspinall's pictures and the museum down

131
00:08:24,693 --> 00:08:28,350
the road are how most people
can see the square today.
